\section{The G-MFRM Framework}
\label{sec:method}

Section~\ref{sec:confounding} established that omitting contamination from the measurement model produces directional bias in ability estimates (Observation~\ref{obs:ovb}). We now introduce the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which jointly estimates ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility within a single measurement framework. The key design choices, staged estimation with externally fixed exposure and a regularized horseshoe prior for the contamination term, are motivated by the identifiability analysis in \S\ref{sec:identifiability} and the error propagation bounds in \S\ref{sec:error_prop}.

\subsection{Model Specification}
\label{sec:model_spec}

G-MFRM extends the standard MFRM \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm} by adding a multiplicative contamination gating term to the linear predictor (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}):
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gmfrm}
\log \frac{P(Y_{nij} \geq k)}{P(Y_{nij} < k)} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i,
\end{equation}
%
where all terms carry the definitions from Section~\ref{sec:confounding}. The gating structure $c_n \cdot e_i$ separates the model-specific and item-specific components of contamination: $c_n \geq 0$ captures how much model $n$ benefits from memorized benchmark content, while $e_i \in [0,1]$ quantifies how much of item $i$ has leaked into training corpora. When $c_n = 0$ (a clean model) or $e_i = 0$ (a novel item), the gating term vanishes and Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm} reduces to the standard MFRM. This graceful degradation ensures that G-MFRM imposes no distortion on uncontaminated model-item pairs.

We enforce non-negativity and sparsity of $c_n$ through a regularized horseshoe prior \citep{piironen2017horseshoe}. A global shrinkage scale $\tau_c \sim \text{HalfCauchy}(1.0)$ controls the overall contamination level, per-model local scales $\lambda_n \sim \text{HalfCauchy}(1.0)$ allow adaptive shrinkage, and a slab component $c^2_{\mathrm{slab}} \sim \text{InverseGamma}(2.0, 8.0)$ prevents unbounded estimates. The effective contamination is $c_n = |\tau_c \cdot \tilde{\lambda}_n \cdot c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n|$, where $\tilde{\lambda}_n$ is the regularized local scale. This prior strongly shrinks $c_n$ toward zero for clean models while permitting large values for genuinely contaminated ones, reflecting the expectation that contamination is sparse across the model population. The remaining priors are $\theta_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $d_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.5)$, and standard uniform on thresholds $\tau_k$.

The closest antecedent is the Doubly-Gated IRT model of \citet{shu2013dgirt}, which introduced a binary gating mechanism to handle item preknowledge in educational testing. G-MFRM differs in three respects. First, exposure $e_i$ is continuous rather than binary, allowing partial contamination to be modeled. Second, the multi-facet structure incorporates judge severity $d_j$ as an explicit nuisance parameter, whereas DG-IRT assumes a single-rater design. Third, exposure is externally estimated rather than treated as known, necessitating the staged procedure and error propagation analysis that follow.

\subsection{Staged Estimation}
\label{sec:staged}

Joint estimation of all parameters in Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm}, including $e_i$, would require identifying the bilinear product $c_n \cdot e_i$ from response data alone. This creates a fundamental non-identifiability (Proposition~\ref{prop:exposure_variation}). We resolve this through a three-stage procedure that fixes exposure externally before estimating the contamination parameters.

\paragraph{Stage 1: Standard MFRM.} We fit the standard MFRM (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}) via MCMC (NUTS) to obtain initial estimates $(\hat{\theta}, \hat{b}, \hat{d})$. These serve as warm-start values for the sampler in Stage~3 and improve convergence.

\paragraph{Stage 2: Exposure estimation.} Per-item exposure $e_i$ is estimated from external evidence independent of the response data used in Stages~1 and~3. The primary signal is \emph{reference model anchoring}: using a fixed reference model (e.g., gpt-4-0314) as anchor, we compute win-rate residuals for each target model against difficulty-adjusted expectations; items where target models systematically outperform the anchor beyond what difficulty predicts are flagged as potentially exposed. The secondary signal is \emph{cross-model agreement}: items receiving uniformly high scores across architecturally diverse models suggest exposure rather than genuine easiness, quantified via $z$-score standardization of the inter-model agreement distribution. These signals are combined as a weighted composite (75\% anchoring, 25\% agreement), clipped to $[0, 1]$, and treated as fixed in Stage~3. Where model log-probabilities are accessible, LogProber can provide an additional gray-box memorization signal, though it is not used in our current experiments.

\paragraph{Stage 3: Joint MCMC.} With $e_i$ fixed, we sample $(\theta_n, b_i, d_j, c_n)$ jointly via NUTS \citep{betancourt2015}. The priors are as specified in \S\ref{sec:model_spec}. For $c_n$, the regularized horseshoe prior \citep{piironen2017horseshoe} computes the regularized local scale as
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:horseshoe}
\tilde{\lambda}_n^2 = \frac{c^2_{\mathrm{slab}} \cdot \lambda_n^2}{c^2_{\mathrm{slab}} + \tau_c^2 \cdot \lambda_n^2}, \quad c_n = |\tau_c \cdot \tilde{\lambda}_n \cdot c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n|,
\end{equation}
%
where $c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$. The slab component bounds the maximum effect size, while the HalfCauchy local scales $\lambda_n$ provide adaptive shrinkage that concentrates clean models near $c_n = 0$ without attenuating genuinely contaminated ones.

Convergence is assessed via the split-$\hat{R}$ statistic ($\hat{R} < 1.05$) and effective sample size (ESS $> 200$) across all chains.\footnote{Synthetic validation with $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$ yields max $\hat{R} = 1.02$ and min ESS $= 276$ across all parameters (exp-012).}


\subsection{Identifiability}
\label{sec:identifiability}

Adding a multiplicative term to a measurement model raises the question of whether the new parameters can be separated from the existing ones. We establish that fixing $e_i$ externally is both necessary and sufficient for identification. All proofs assume the standard location constraint $\sum_n \theta_n = 0$.

\begin{proposition}[No New Indeterminacy]
\label{prop:no_new}
When $e_i$ is fixed externally, the gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$ introduces no additional location or scale indeterminacy beyond that already present in the standard MFRM.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $c_n' = c_n + \delta$ preserves all response probabilities. Then $\delta \cdot e_i = 0$ for all items. Since $e_i \neq 0$ for at least one item by assumption, $\delta = 0$. The key observation is that fixing $e_i$ externally eliminates the bilinear ambiguity $c_n \cdot e_i = (\lambda c_n)(\lambda^{-1} e_i)$ that would otherwise render the decomposition non-unique.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exposure Variation Necessary]
\label{prop:exposure_variation}
If $e_i = e$ for all $i$ (constant exposure), then $c_n$ and $\theta_n$ are not separately identifiable.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
When exposure is constant, the linear predictor becomes $(\theta_n + c_n \cdot e) - b_i - d_j - \tau_k$, which is equivalent to a standard MFRM with reparameterized ability $\theta^*_n = \theta_n + c_n \cdot e$. No configuration of response data can distinguish $(\theta_n, c_n)$ from $(\theta_n + \epsilon, c_n - \epsilon/e)$ for any $\epsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\mathrm{Var}(e_i) > 0$ is a necessary condition for identifiability of $c_n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sufficient Conditions for Local Identifiability]
\label{thm:sufficient}
The G-MFRM is locally identifiable, up to the standard location constraint, if the following conditions hold: (i) a fully crossed design (all $N \times I \times J$ cells observed), (ii) $J \geq 2$ judges, (iii) at least two distinct exposure values $|\{e_i\}| \geq 2$, (iv) $\mathrm{Var}(e_i) > 0$, and (v) standard normalization $\sum_n \theta_n = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Following the approach of \citet{rothenberg1971} and \citet{sanmartin2016}, local identifiability holds if the Fisher information matrix is non-singular at the true parameter values. The critical block is the $(\theta_n, c_n)$ submatrix for each model $n$. Its determinant takes the form
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fisher_det}
\det \mathbf{F}_n = \Bigl(\textstyle\sum_{i,j} w_{nij}\Bigr)\Bigl(\textstyle\sum_{i,j} w_{nij} e_i^2\Bigr) - \Bigl(\textstyle\sum_{i,j} w_{nij} e_i\Bigr)^2,
\end{equation}
%
where $w_{nij} > 0$ are the Fisher information weights for each observation. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, this determinant is strictly positive whenever $e_i$ is not constant across items, which is guaranteed by conditions (iii) and (iv). The remaining blocks for $b_i$ and $d_j$ inherit non-singularity from the standard MFRM under conditions (i), (ii), and (v).
\end{proof}

Conditions (i) and (ii) are design requirements satisfied by standard multi-judge evaluation protocols. Conditions (iii) and (iv) are the substantive constraints: the benchmark must contain items with varying levels of exposure. In practice, any benchmark containing both frequently leaked items (e.g., MMLU questions circulated online) and novel items satisfies these conditions.

\subsection{Error Propagation}
\label{sec:error_prop}

The staged procedure treats the Stage~2 exposure estimates $\hat{e}_i$ as fixed quantities in Stage~3. This is a generated regressors problem \citep{pagan1984}: measurement error in $\hat{e}_i$ propagates into the posterior of $(\theta_n, c_n)$. The multiplicative structure $c_n \cdot e_i$ makes this an errors-in-variables (EIV) problem \citep{fuller1987}, with a predictable attenuation pattern.

Let $\hat{e}_i = e_i + \epsilon_i$ with $\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ independent of all model parameters. The attenuation factor for the contamination coefficient is
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:attenuation}
\lambda(\sigma) = \frac{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}{\mathrm{Var}(e_i) + \sigma^2},
\end{equation}
%
which implies a critical noise threshold $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}} = \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}$ at which attenuation reaches 50\%. In the synthetic DGP of exp-012, $\mathrm{Var}(e_i) \approx 0.04$, yielding $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}} \approx 0.2$.

%% exp-012 data: error propagation sensitivity
Figure~\ref{fig:error_prop} and Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} show the empirical degradation across noise levels. Below the critical threshold ($\sigma \leq 0.2$), degradation is mild: $\theta$-RMSE increases by 5.9\% (from $0.128 \pm 0.018$ to $0.135 \pm 0.018$) and $c_n$-RMSE by 7.9\% (from $0.533 \pm 0.187$ to $0.575 \pm 0.198$). At $\sigma = 0.1$, the increases are only 1.4\% and 2.3\% respectively. Beyond the threshold ($\sigma = 0.5$), degradation becomes severe: $\theta$-RMSE rises 36.9\% and $c_n$-RMSE rises 57.9\%. Contamination estimates $c_n$ are more sensitive than ability estimates $\theta_n$ because $c_n$ enters the likelihood only through its product with $e_i$ (multiplicative coupling), while $\theta_n$ contributes additively and is anchored by all observations regardless of exposure.

These bounds make the staged approximation falsifiable: if the noise level of the Stage~2 exposure estimates can be bounded below $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}}$, the resulting ability estimates carry quantifiable error guarantees. For honest credible intervals that account for generated-regressor uncertainty, we apply the Murphy-Topel variance correction \citep{murphy1985} to inflate the Stage~3 posterior variance by the estimated Stage~2 sampling variability.

\subsection{Fair Scores and Diagnostics}
\label{sec:fair_scores}

The Stage~3 posterior mean of $\theta_n$ provides a bias-and-contamination-corrected ``fair score'' for each model. The remaining parameters serve complementary diagnostic roles: per-model $c_n$ flags candidates for training data audit, per-judge $d_j$ informs judge calibration, and per-item $e_i$ identifies items for retirement or replacement.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation increasingly relies on LLM-as-a-judge protocols, where one or more LLM judges score model responses on structured benchmarks \citep{zheng2023mtbench}. Two well-documented sources of distortion affect these evaluations: \emph{judge bias}, where different judges assign systematically different scores to the same response, and \emph{benchmark contamination}, where models achieve inflated scores on items encountered during training. Both problems have received sustained research attention, producing separate toolkits for bias calibration and contamination detection. These toolkits treat the two distortions as independent phenomena, a separation that reflects their origins in different research communities rather than any principled argument for independence. Accurate ability ranking is essential for model selection, safety evaluation, and benchmark design; systematic distortion in estimated abilities undermines all three. Yet a practitioner who needs both corrections must apply them sequentially: first correct one source of distortion, then the other. This sequential approach produces unreliable ability estimates. In synthetic experiments with 50 models, 100 items, and 8 judges, sequential estimation yields ability root mean squared error (RMSE) of $0.221 \pm 0.038$, while joint estimation achieves $0.124 \pm 0.016$, a 43.8\% reduction (Wilcoxon $p < 10^{-6}$). The gap does not arise from estimation noise or algorithmic limitations of individual methods; it reflects structural confounding between judge bias and contamination that no sequential pipeline can resolve.

Existing judge bias correction methods remove systematic differences across judges without modeling contamination. CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026} uses round-robin pairing to cancel average bias through balanced exposure of model pairs to all judges, but treats all residual score variation as genuine ability signal. UDA \citep{uda2026} learns a neural K-factor adjustment that reduces inter-judge standard deviation by 63.4\%, yet operates as a black-box transformation with no mechanism for contamination effects. On the contamination side, ConStat \citep{constat2024} compares target model performance against a reference model to estimate item-level contamination ($r^2 = 0.94$ with true contamination levels in controlled settings), DCR \citep{dcr2025} applies fuzzy inference to adjust scores on suspected leaked items, and LogProber \citep{logprober2024} probes token-level log-probabilities to detect memorization signatures. These two families of methods operate in separate silos. A bias-first pipeline absorbs contamination-induced score inflation into the ability estimates, because the bias model attributes all residual systematic variation to genuine ability differences. A contamination-first pipeline misattributes judge severity differences as contamination signals, because score elevation from a lenient judge is indistinguishable from score elevation due to memorization without a joint model. In either ordering, the first correction stage produces estimates already distorted by the unmodeled facet, and the second stage inherits this distortion with no mechanism for self-correction.

We formalize this confounding as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem \citep{angrist2009mostly}. Within the Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) framework \citep{linacre1989mfrm}, the complete data-generating process includes both judge severity $d_j$ and a contamination interaction $c_n \cdot e_i$. Fitting a specification that includes one facet but omits the other produces asymptotically biased parameter estimates whenever the two facets are non-orthogonal. This non-orthogonality holds in practice: lenient judges (low severity $d_j$) amplify score inflation on exposed items, while strict judges compress it, creating systematic dependence between judge effects and contamination effects. The OVB framework predicts that the bias magnitude scales with the contamination susceptibility $c_n$ of each model, so the most contaminated models receive the largest upward distortion in estimated ability. For practical benchmark evaluations, this means that the more a model's training data overlaps with the benchmark, the more its ability is overestimated, precisely the failure mode that contamination correction is designed to prevent. Synthetic validation confirms the predicted pattern: the Pearson correlation between sequential estimation bias and true $c_n$ reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$), which shows that contaminated models are systematically overestimated under both sequential orderings (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which combines the multi-facet structure of MFRM \citep{linacre1989mfrm, jiao2025mfrm_llm} with the gating mechanism of Doubly-Gated Item Response Theory (DG-IRT) \citep{shu2013dgirt} to jointly estimate model ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility in a single measurement framework. G-MFRM extends the MFRM linear predictor with a multiplicative contamination gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$, where $c_n \geq 0$ captures model-specific contamination susceptibility and $e_i \in [0,1]$ quantifies per-item exposure. When $c_n = 0$ or $e_i = 0$, the gating term vanishes and the model reduces to standard MFRM, so uncontaminated model-item pairs incur no distortion. Because the bilinear product $c_n \cdot e_i$ cannot be uniquely decomposed from response data alone, G-MFRM employs a three-stage calibration procedure: (1) standard MFRM warm-start to obtain initial parameter estimates, (2) external per-item exposure estimation from evidence independent of the response data, and (3) joint Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling with $e_i$ fixed from Stage~2. We provide a formal identifiability analysis (Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new}--\ref{prop:exposure_variation} and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) that proves fixing $e_i$ externally is both necessary and sufficient for parameter separation under standard multi-judge designs (Section~\ref{sec:method}). A complementary error propagation analysis bounds the impact of Stage~2 measurement noise: when exposure estimation error satisfies $\sigma \leq 0.2$, ability RMSE degradation remains below 6\%. Figure~\ref{fig:framework} provides an overview of the complete framework.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We formalize the confounding between judge bias and benchmark contamination in LLM evaluation as an omitted variable bias problem, and show that sequential correction produces directional bias proportional to model contamination levels (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}, Figure~\ref{fig:ovb_bias}).
    \item We propose the G-MFRM framework with formal identifiability guarantees (Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new}--\ref{prop:exposure_variation} and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) and quantified error propagation bounds for the staged estimation procedure (Section~\ref{sec:method}, Figure~\ref{fig:error_prop}, Table~\ref{tab:error_prop}).
    \item Synthetic validation confirms that joint estimation reduces ability RMSE by 43.8\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 10^{-6}$), with Kendall rank correlation $\tau = 0.922$ versus $\tau = 0.859$ (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
    \item Empirical validation on real-data evaluation with 9 LLM judges and 9 target models on Arena-Hard, comparing G-MFRM fair scores against human preference rankings from Chatbot Arena (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}). %% [Phase 2: numbers TBD]
\end{enumerate}

